        "original_id": "8670d0b5-5bb3-51e2-8a4d-75d5a1383c48",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Lowell George, Ian Hunter, Christopher Cross\n- Bands: Little Feat, Mott the Hoople\n- Songs and Albums: Ride Like the Wind, Dixie Chicken\n\nFacts:\n- Ian Hunter Patterson, known as Ian Hunter, was born on 3 June 1939 and became the lead singer of the English rock band Mott the Hoople from its 1969 inception to its 1974 dissolution.\n- Lowell Thomas George was born on April 13, 1945 and was an American songwriter, multi-instrumentalist, record producer, and the primary guitarist, vocalist, and songwriter for Little Feat.\n- Little Feat released its third studio album, Dixie Chicken, in 1973.\n- Ian Hunter returned as lead singer for Mott the Hoople reunions in 2009 and 2013.\n- Lowell George died on June 29, 1979.\n- Christopher Cross dedicated the song \"Ride Like the Wind\" on the album's inner sleeve to Lowell George of Little Feat after George died in 1979.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "07ab3d55-c8f2-53e7-9057-18527af7333e",
        "text": "Page: Lowell George. Sentence: Lowell Thomas George (April 13, 1945 – June 29, 1979) was an American songwriter, multi-instrumentalist, and record producer, who was the primary guitarist, vocalist, and songwriter for the rock band Little Feat.\nPage: Ian Hunter (singer). Sentence: Ian Hunter Patterson (born 3 June 1939), known as Ian Hunter, is a British singer-songwriter who is best known as the lead singer of the English rock band Mott the Hoople, from its inception in 1969 to its dissolution in 1974, and at the time of its 2009 and 2013 reunions.\nPage: Ride Like the Wind. Sentence: On the album's inner sleeve, Christopher Cross dedicated this song to Lowell George, formerly of the band Little Feat, who had died in 1979.\nPage: Dixie Chicken (album). Sentence: Dixie Chicken is the third studio album by the American rock band Little Feat, released in 1973.",
